  "query_preview": "/MONDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 219 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nMonday night through Tuesday will need to be watched for the \npotential of isolated strong to severe storms over the northern \nportions of our region as additional shortwave impulses ride the top \nof the heat dome. This severe storms risk appears to be conditional, \nand thus, confidence is low for storms to become severe. However, \ngiven sufficient MUCAPE values around 2000-3000 J/kg, combined with \n25 to 35 knots of 0-6 km shear, the convective parameter space would \nbe supportive of strong/severe storms late Monday night and again \nTuesday afternoon if anything develops. Overall, the large scale \nforcing from these shortwaves remains pretty weak, so SPC continues \nto have only a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe storms for \nthese time frames. The Tuesday afternoon storm potential will have \nthe potential to limit daytime heating, and thus, limiting the heat \nindices. Storms will be most likely north of I-80 for each time \nframe.\n\nOutside of the storm threat, we will continue to have hot and humid \nconditions over at least the southern portions of the area where \nstorm chances are much more limited through much of the week. \nConfidence in the hot conditions Tuesday through Thursday remain \nhigh, given the latest ECMWF EFI/Shift of Tails values for high \ntemperatures Tuesday through Thursday are in excess of 0.8 to 0.9 \n(even some values over 0.95 in isolated spots), along with Shift of \nTails values around 1. This suggests high confidence in anomalously \nwarm high temperatures, although not extreme values based on the \nlower Shift of Tails. With the potential for these anomalously hot \nconditions to last this week, we've decided to have the Heat \nAdvisory go through the daylight hours Wednesday. It's entirely \npossible for the advisory to be extended into Thursday, given LREF \nensemble exceedance probabilities of 100 degree heat indices \napproaching 50% across our southern two tiers of counties. \nAdditionally, overnight lows might not dip below 75 degrees Monday \nnight through Wednesday night, so this would compound the heat \nstress - even at night. There is potential for some record warm low \ntemperatures during this period. See the Climate section below for \nthe current records.",
